::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Why are definitions important in Geometry? {
=They give exact meanings for mathematical words. #Correct. Definitions help students use Geometry language consistently and precisely.
~They make diagrams unnecessary. #Definitions support diagram reasoning, but diagrams can still provide important information.
~They allow students to guess from appearance. #Definitions reduce guessing by identifying the features that matter.
~They replace the need for explanations. #Definitions support explanations, but students still need to connect definitions to evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which object is an exact location usually named with a capital letter? {
~Line #A line is a straight path that extends forever in both directions.
=Point #Correct. A point is an exact location and is usually named with a capital letter.
~Segment #A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
~Angle #An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A straight path extends forever in both directions. Which object is described? {
=Line #Correct. A line extends forever in both directions.
~Ray #A ray extends in one direction from one endpoint, not in both directions.
~Segment #A segment has two endpoints and does not extend forever.
~Point #A point is a location, not an extended straight path.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which object has one endpoint and extends forever in one direction? {
~Line #A line extends forever in both directions and has no endpoints.
=Ray #Correct. A ray has one endpoint and extends forever in one direction.
~Segment #A segment has two endpoints.
~Angle #An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which object has two endpoints? {
~Point #A point is a single location and does not have two endpoints.
~Line #A line extends forever in both directions and has no endpoints.
~Ray #A ray has one endpoint.
=Segment #Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which object is formed by two rays with a common endpoint? {
~Line #A line is one straight path extending in both directions.
~Segment #A segment has two endpoints and is not formed by two rays.
=Angle #Correct. An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint.
~Point #A point is a location, not a figure formed by rays.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A figure begins at point M and continues through point N in one direction. Which description is most precise? {
~Line MN #A line extends in both directions and has no endpoint.
=Ray MN #Correct. Ray MN starts at M and continues through N in one direction.
~Segment MN #A segment has two endpoints, but the figure continues in one direction.
~Angle MN #An angle is formed by two rays and usually requires three letters to name clearly.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A figure has endpoints R and S. Which description is most precise? {
~Line RS #A line has no endpoints and extends in both directions.
~Ray RS #A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction.
=Segment RS #Correct. A segment has two endpoints, so endpoints R and S identify segment RS.
~Angle RS #An angle is formed by two rays with a common endpoint and is not named with only two letters here.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Rays PA and PB share endpoint P. Which statement is most precise? {
=Rays PA and PB form angle APB with vertex P. #Correct. This statement names the rays, angle, and vertex precisely.
~There is a corner near P. #This is informal and does not identify the rays or angle.
~PA and PB are the same segment. #Rays are not segments, and two different rays are being described.
~P is just part of the drawing. #This does not explain the geometric relationship or the shared endpoint.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>In angle DEF, which point is the vertex? {
~D #D is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the middle letter.
=E #Correct. The middle letter names the vertex, so E is the vertex of angle DEF.
~F #F is on one ray of the angle, but it is not the vertex.
~D and F #D and F help name the rays, but the shared endpoint is E.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is vague rather than precise? {
~Segment AB has endpoints A and B. #This uses precise language and identifies the endpoints.
~Ray CD begins at C and passes through D. #This describes the ray precisely.
=The figure has a corner over there. #Correct. This is vague because it does not identify the object, point, rays, or angle.
~Line EF extends in both directions. #This uses precise language about a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student says, "This is a line because it looks straight." What is missing from the reasoning? {
~A color description #Color is not needed to identify a line.
~A guess about length #Guessing length does not define a line.
=Evidence that the figure extends in both directions #Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions, so extension evidence matters.
~A statement that it has two endpoints #Two endpoints would describe a segment, not a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student calls a figure a segment because it looks short. What misconception is shown? {
~The student used endpoints correctly. #No endpoints are mentioned. The student relied on appearance.
=The student used appearance instead of a definition. #Correct. A segment is identified by two endpoints, not by how short the drawing looks.
~The student identified a vertex. #A vertex is not part of this reasoning.
~The student proved the figure was a line. #The student did not prove anything about a line.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which sentence correctly revises "The shape has a corner at B"? {
~The thing turns at B. #This remains vague and does not name a geometric object.
~B is where it bends. #This is informal and does not identify rays or an angle.
=Rays BA and BC form angle ABC with vertex B. #Correct. This precise sentence names the rays, the angle, and the vertex.
~B looks like the middle. #This relies on appearance and does not use precise Geometry language.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best explains the difference between observation and evidence? {
~An observation is always proof. #An observation may help, but proof or conclusion requires supporting evidence.
=An observation is something noticed; evidence is information used to support a conclusion. #Correct. Evidence uses observations, labels, definitions, markings, or given statements to support a conclusion.
~Evidence is only a guess from the picture. #Evidence is not a guess; it supports a conclusion.
~Observation and evidence mean the same thing in Geometry. #They are related, but they are not the same.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which option is evidence that a figure is a ray? {
~It looks shorter than the other figures. #Length by appearance does not define a ray.
~It is drawn near the top of the page. #Location on the page does not define a geometric object.
=It has one endpoint and an arrow showing extension in one direction. #Correct. These features match the definition of a ray.
~It has two endpoints and no arrows. #That describes a segment, not a ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which option is evidence that a figure is a segment? {
~It has arrows on both ends. #Arrows on both ends suggest a line, not a segment.
=It has two endpoints. #Correct. A segment is a part of a line with two endpoints.
~It has one endpoint and one arrow. #That describes a ray.
~It is drawn horizontally. #Orientation does not define a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which option is evidence that a figure is a line? {
~It has one endpoint. #A figure with one endpoint is not a line.
~It has two endpoints. #A figure with two endpoints is a segment.
~It looks long. #Length by appearance does not define a line.
=It is straight and has arrows showing it extends in both directions. #Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement uses a definition correctly? {
~A line has two endpoints. #A line has no endpoints and extends in both directions.
~A segment extends forever in one direction. #A segment has two endpoints and does not extend forever.
=A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction. #Correct. This is the definition of a ray.
~A point is formed by two rays. #An angle is formed by two rays; a point is an exact location.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement uses appearance instead of evidence? {
~Segment AB has endpoints A and B. #This uses endpoint evidence.
~Ray CD has endpoint C and extends through D. #This uses defining features of a ray.
~Line EF has arrows on both ends. #This uses diagram evidence for extension in both directions.
=The figure is a segment because it looks short. #Correct. This relies on appearance instead of the defining feature of two endpoints.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is segment JK"? {
~It is near the center of the diagram. #Position in the diagram does not support segment identification.
~It looks like a small line. #Appearance is not enough. The definition matters.
=The figure has endpoints J and K. #Correct. A segment has two endpoints, so this evidence supports the conclusion.
~It passes through point J. #Passing through one point is not enough to identify a segment.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is ray TU"? {
~It has endpoints T and U. #Two endpoints describe a segment, not a ray.
=It starts at T and continues through U in one direction. #Correct. A ray has one endpoint and extends through another point in one direction.
~It extends in both directions through T and U. #That describes a line, not a ray.
~It is the shortest figure shown. #Length by appearance does not identify a ray.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best supports the conclusion "the figure is line VW"? {
~It has endpoints V and W. #Endpoints identify a segment, not a line.
~It starts at V and ends at W. #A line does not start or end at those points.
~It looks straight and short. #Looking straight is not enough, and shortness does not define a line.
=It is straight and extends in both directions through V and W. #Correct. A line is straight and extends forever in both directions.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes, "A ray has two endpoints." What is the correct revision? {
~A ray has no endpoints and extends in both directions. #That describes a line, not a ray.
=A ray has one endpoint and extends in one direction. #Correct. This is the definition of a ray.
~A ray is formed by two rays with a common endpoint. #That describes an angle.
~A ray is an exact location. #That describes a point.
}

::GEO_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only - No primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping<br><br>Question:<br>What is the main purpose of this readiness lesson? {
~To assign a formal Geometry benchmark #This lesson is readiness support only and does not introduce a new primary MLA standard.
~To prove advanced theorems about figures #Formal proof develops later. This lesson builds language and definition habits first.
=To use precise definitions and evidence when describing geometric objects #Correct. The lesson focuses on precise language, definitions, observation, evidence, and avoiding appearance-based conclusions.
~To replace diagrams with memorized formulas #This lesson uses diagrams and definitions, not formula memorization.
}
